+# Supplied ZebPay collection audit
+
+Audited **19 October 2026**, before implementation.
+
+- Source: `Zebpay API Documentation.postman_collection(1).json`
+- SHA-256: `0462b41646c31a25cc9580b873d91eb57c42072346807e86b7895795f4defdbd`
+- 38 requests: 27 GET, 8 POST, 3 DELETE.
+- 30 saved responses; 58 collection/request event definitions.
+- Entire JSON parsed: all Spot and Futures folders, descriptions, request headers/auth, query parameters, raw bodies, variable definitions, scripts, original requests, and every response/array item inspected.
+- No outside exchange documentation, endpoint, authentication recipe, or alternate exchange is used.
+
+The reproducible, privacy-preserving inventory is [collection-audit.json](collection-audit.json). No sample account balances, positions, API tokens, or account IDs were copied into runtime data or the frontend.
+
+## Authentication findings
+
+### Spot (Trade API folder): documented
+
+The **Trade API folder description** contains separate signing recipes:
+
+- GET: insert/replace `timestamp` using `Date.now()` (**milliseconds**); encode query keys/values with JavaScript `encodeURIComponent`, in insertion order; HMAC-SHA256 the resulting query with the API secret; lowercase hexadecimal signature.
+- POST: parse the raw JSON payload, set `timestamp = Date.now()`, `JSON.stringify` it, HMAC-SHA256 those **exact transmitted bytes**; lowercase hexadecimal signature.
+- Headers: `X-AUTH-APIKEY`, `X-AUTH-SIGNATURE`, `Content-Type: application/json`.
+- No DELETE signing recipe is provided. The presence of a query timestamp does not establish that GET signing applies to DELETE.
+
+The collection-wide pre-request script generates a seconds timestamp and a UUID `requestId`. The explicit Spot signing recipes overwrite the timestamp in milliseconds; the implementation follows those recipes. It **does not add** the global UUID to order bodies: no Spot request defines a client idempotency field. App-side UUIDs exist only in local paper accounting.
+
+### Futures: not independently verified
+
+Futures is a **sibling** of Trade API, not a child inheriting its description. It has no signature-generation recipe of its own. In particular:
+
+- Several requests use X-AUTH headers, while the Futures order prose also explicitly requires a Bearer access token.
+- `ExchangePairs` explicitly has a Bearer header; there is no access-token issuance/refresh operation.
+- Futures wallet has signed headers but no query timestamp.
+- History endpoints describe `timestamp` as a pagination/update cursor (initially `null`), while some raw requests use the collection timestamp variable.
+- Some prose schemas and saved responses disagree about arrays, nested `data`, or wallet fields.
+- The order prose mentions LIMIT, but the actual raw order body is MARKET and does not establish a verified complete LIMIT format.
+
+**Result:** no authenticated Futures reads, orders, cancellation, margin changes, leverage changes, or private P&L. Reusing Spot signing, acquiring a token elsewhere, or borrowing conventions from another exchange would be guessing.
+
+## Base-URL handling: no implicit casing repair
+
+Only `baseurl` is defined, as `https://www.zebapi.com/api/v1`. Many requests instead use undefined `baseUrl`.
+
+- Public Spot market list: the saved response's `originalRequest` provides the concrete URL. That is sufficient evidence to use it.
+- Pending/completed orders and the four Spot create-order examples: concrete originalRequest URLs are supplied.
+- BTC-INR ticker: the defined lowercase `baseurl` and exact `group=singapore` are supplied.
+- Spot book: both request and example retain undefined `baseUrl`; no automatic alias is introduced. The request is not called. The prose reference to `book_long` is not promoted into a new implementation.
+- Spot balances/fees/fills/pairs also lack complete response contracts, independently of the unresolved base variable.
+
+This is deliberately conservative. A future corrected collection can resolve the ambiguity explicitly.
+
